#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""mmx-scan — M-606/M-608: data feed and measurement for the MMX self-improvement loop.

All modes are read-only on Multica and MOCT (they never change issues,
comments, memory or agents; acting on the output is the curator agent's job).
`nightly` writes only to the git checkout it is given. M-608 modes
(snapshot, export, nightly, compare, chart) are documented in README.md.
The two M-606 modes:

  mmx-scan baseline [--days 7] [--markdown]
      Weekly baseline over `multica issue run-messages` (M-571 metric):
      share of completed runs that loaded the `mmx` skill, memory_search /
      memory_get / memory_note call counts, "Harness edit:" proposals, and
      team convention/rule records created in the window. Baseline to beat
      (M-571, 2026-11-25..27): 1/429 completed runs loaded `mmx`.

  mmx-scan signals [--hours 24] [--markdown]
      Daily correction signals for the curator autopilot: reopens, backward
      status moves (Needs changes), moves to blocked, plan_verdict runs,
      REQUEST_CHANGES / reject comments, Owner (member) comments, cards with
      >=2 failed runs, and pending MOCT records waiting for approval.
      Owner comments are CANDIDATES: the curator decides which are corrections.

Detection rules (baseline):
  * mmx loaded = a `Skill` tool call naming `mmx`, a file-read tool on a path
    containing `mmx/SKILL.md`, or a shell `cat/sed/head/...` of that file.
    A grep or script that only names the path is not counted.
  * memory_* = tool_use whose tool name contains `memory_search`,
    `memory_get` or `memory_note` (covers `mcp__moct__memory_note` etc.).
  * Harness edit = assistant text or a non-shell tool input (comment file
    write) with a line starting "Harness edit:", except the skill template
    "Harness edit: <layer>".

The curator's own autopilot runs are excluded from the baseline ratio
(`--curator-agent`, default Opus Architect) so the loop does not inflate its
own metric.

Only stdlib. Uses "$MULTICA_CLI_PATH" (falls back to `multica`). MOCT records
are read through `multica moct-mcp` (stdio JSON-RPC); if moct-be is not
reachable the MOCT fields report "unavailable" instead of failing the scan.
"""

